Many technical efforts have been made to detect trace-level xylene. Standard analysis methods for xylene detection are based on bulky instruments like gas chromatography (GC).5−7 In addition to expensive, laborious, and time-consuming, a complicated sampling process for preconcentration is needed prior to GC analysis.5 On the other hand, the bulky GC instrument cannot be used for on-site measurement, which hinders the wide applications for xylene detection. Therefore, to meet the fast and on-site detection requirements, it is highly demanded to develop portable devices like high performance gas sensor to detect and monitor ambient xylene.8−11
Resonant microcantilever has drawn intensive attention in the application fields of trace-level detection of gas molecules.12−14 For gas molecule detection, specific sensing material is loaded at the end of the resonant microcantilever. The added mass of the adsorbed gas molecules on the cantilever directly brings resonating frequency shift of the microcantilever, and the frequency signal is proportional to the adsorbed mass. Besides the very high mass sensing resolution (normally finer than $^\mathrm{~1~}\mathrm{pg})$ that is attributed to the tiny cantilever structure and the high resonant Q-factor, the resonant microcantilevers feature merits of low fabrication cost and high batch uniformity, because the silicon integrated cantilevers can be fabricated with microelectromechanical system (MEMS) techniques.12 To date, numerous functional materials, such as mesoporous silica,15 graphene oxide,16 hyperbranched polymer/copolymer,17,18 and metal−organic frameworks (MOFs),19,20 have been successfully utilized as the resonant-gravimetric sensing materials, and the successfully detected gases include explosives, amines, ethanol, hexane, and carbon dioxide. Generally, those organic chemicals are polar molecules that are relatively easy to detect. Unfortunately, it is still a big challenge to detect inert molecules, such as xylene, especially at the trace level of subppm by using gas sensors. Due to the chemical inertness of the xylene molecule, its sensing material is difficult to be developed. For example, by using another kind of resonant transducer of quartz crystal microbalance (QCM), Du et al. reported a copolymer sensing material for xylene detection. The copolymer-coated QCM sensor experimentally exhibited the lowest detectable concentration of $\mathsf{\Pi}_{54}\mathsf{\Pi}_{\mathsf{P P}}\mathsf{m},\mathsf{\Omega}^{21}$ which is still about 2 orders of magnitude higher than the human’s olfactory threshold of $0.47\mathrm{ppm}$ . Thus, ultrasensitive xylene sensing materials need to be developed to detect xylene at low concentration levels.
It is herein considered that MOF-based materials should rightfully have significant advantages in resonant-gravimetric detection of trace xylene. The nanoporous structure of MOF provides huge specific surface area. As is reported in literatures, its specific surface area can reach 7140 m2/g.22,23 Much more importantly, due to the electron acceptor (known as typical Lewis acid) function of metal ion units in MOFs such as $\bar{Z}\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+},$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ , there exists specific interaction between the electron donor of xylene and the metal sites in MOFs. In addition, it should be mentioned that many kinds of MOF materials contain common building blocks of the benzene ring, which can adsorb xylene molecules through the interaction of “like dissolves like” between the MOFs and the guest molecules of xylene. We confirm this by simulation with the Sorption Module in the Materials Studio software. As a proof of concept, the typical MOF material of $\mathrm{{Cu}}_{3}(\mathrm{{BTC})}_{2}$ (copper benzene tricarboxylate, also known as HKUST-1)24−26 is selected for simulation of the host−guest interaction with xylene molecules. The simulated result in Figure 1 clearly shows that the adsorption sites of HKUST-1 to xylene molecules consist of two parts: ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ and benzene ring building blocks. Based on all the above, MOF is reasonably assumed as potential sensing material for the benzene-homologue of xylene.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Chemicals. Trimesic acid, terephthalic acid, $p$ -xylene, $^{4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime}}$ -benzene-1,3,5-triyltribenzoate(BTB), 2-methylimidazole, $\mathrm{Cu}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O},$ , zinc acetate, trimethylamine, $^{N,N-}$ dimethylformamide (DMF), and $_{N,N}$ -diethylformamide (DEF) are purchased from Sigma-Aldrich.
Synthesis of ZIF-8. First, $_{0.61\mathrm{~g~}}$ of $\mathrm{Zn}\mathrm{(NO_{3})}_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is added into $22.60\ \mathrm{g}$ methanol under stirring. After $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}$ · $\mathsf{6H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ is completely dissolved in methanol, stock solution (I) is formed. Meanwhile, $_{1.31\mathrm{~g~2~}}$ -methylimidazole is dissolved completely into $22.0\ \mathrm{g}$ methanol under stirring to form stock solution (II). Then, stock solution (II) is quickly poured into stock solution (I). After further stirring for $\ensuremath{5}\ensuremath{\mathrm{{min}}}$ , the solution is transferred into a Teflon-lined autoclave. The reaction mixture contained Teflon-lined autoclave is heated in an oven at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{\textrm{\scriptsize5h}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the precipitates are collected by centrifugation and washed with methanol for three times. Then, the precipitates are dried at 75 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight, and the ZIF-8 products are finally obtained.
Synthesis of MOF-5. First, $2.60~\mathrm{g}$ terephthalic acid as well as $4.3~\mathrm{mL}$ of triethylamine are together added into $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF under stirring to form stock solution (I). Meanwhile, 8.52 g zinc acetate is dissolved completely into $250~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF under stirring to form stock solution (II). Then, stock solution (II) is slowly added into stock solution (I) and keeps stirring for $2.5\mathrm{h}.$ By using filtration, the solids are collected and further immersed into $250~\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for solvent exchange overnight. After that, the solids are collected and then immersed into 350 mL of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ for another 7 days. Then, the $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ solvent is decanted and the solids are obtained. Thereafter, the solids are dried at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight and the MOF-5 products are finally obtained.
Synthesis of MOF-177. First, $\boldsymbol{0.63\mathrm{~g~}}$ benzenetribenzoic acid and $2.52~\mathrm{g}$ zinc acetate are sequentially added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DEF under stirring for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The solids are collected by centrifugation and washed with DEF three times. After that, the solids are immersed into $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ for 5 days. During the 5 days solvent exchange, the $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ solvent is refreshed three times. Then, the $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ solvent is decanted and the solids are obtained. Finally, the solids are dried at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight and the MOF-177 products are obtained.
Characterization. XRD patterns are obtained by using a Bruker model D8 focus diffractometer equipped with a copper anode to produce X-rays $(40~\mathrm{\kV},~40~\mathrm{\mA})$ ), where the wavelength is $0.154~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ . The data are collected in the continuous scan mode from $5^{\circ}$ to $50^{\circ}$ , with a $0.02^{\circ}$ sampling interval. The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images are taken by using a FEI Tecnai G20 microscope, where $200\mathrm{kV}$ accelerating voltage is used. The morphologies of the assynthesized samples as well as the microcantilever sensor are characterized by using FE-SEM (Hitachi S4800). The accelerating voltages are $1{-}10~\mathrm{~kV}.$ . The nitrogen sorption isotherm is measured at $77~\mathrm{K}$ by using a Micromeritics ASAP $2020\mathbf{M}$ system. Specific surface area and pore size distribution are calculated using Brunauer−Emmett−Teller (BET) and Barrett−Joyner−Halenda (BJH) methods. The FT-IR experiment is performed with a Bruker Vertex $70\mathbf{v}$ infrared spectrometer (under the vacuum $<~100~\mathrm{Pa})$ with a diffuse reflectance accessory. Mercury−cadmium−teluride (MCT) detector is used for FT-IR measurement and the data are recorded from 1000 to $1800~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ . Before FT-IR measurement, about $50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{MOF}$ sample is stuffed into the sample holder and measured as control experiment. Then, the MOF sample is exposed to $50\ \mathrm{ppm}\ p$ -xylene for more than $1~\mathrm{min}$ . After that, the sample is quickly placed into the spectrometer for the in situ measurement. The scan is repeated six times, and the time interval is set as $1~\mathrm{min}$ .
Loading MOF on Cantilever and Sensing Experiment. The MOF material $\left(10{\mathrm{~mg}}\right)$ is added into $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of deionized water (under ultrasonic) to form a crude suspension, which is used as ink in the following material deposition experiment. Then, several drops of the ink are printed onto the microcantilever top-surface by using a commercial GIX II Microplotter (Sonoplot Inc.). Thereafter, the microcantilever is dried in an oven at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for about $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
For sensing properties measurement, the fabricated sensor is prior put into a lab-made testing chamber with volume of $20\mathrm{L}$ . For data acquisition, a commercial frequency counter (Agilent, model 53131A) is connected to the cantilever sensor. Before vapor detection, the frequency value of cantilever sensor is recorded as baseline signal. Then, certain concentration vapor is diluted by injecting certain volume gas into the testing chamber with known volume of $20\mathrm{~L~}$ . After detection, the testing chamber is switching opened to clean air atmosphere.
## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
Based on the Materials Studio simulation results (see Figure 1) and theoretical analysis, four typical MOFs of MOF-5, HKUST-1, ZIF-8, and MOF-177 are synthesized and employed in resonant-gravimetric sensing experiment for detection of trace-level para-xylene ( $p$ -xylene or PX). The phases of the assynthesized four MOFs are identified by using powder X-ray diffraction (XRD), with the results shown in Figure 2, respectively. The sharp diffraction peaks of the corresponding samples are in accordance with the standard XRD patterns that were previously reported.27−30 The results indicate that all four samples with pure phase have been synthesized successfully. The four insets in Figure 2 show four different crystal structures of the four samples from which the metal sites, organic-bridged building blocks (benzene or imidazole rings), and the nanoporous structure can be seen clearly. As is further shown in Figure 3, the tiny nanopores in the four MOF samples can be observed with the help of TEM. Based on the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ sorption experiment, the structural data for specific surface area, porosity, and pore size of the four MOFs are obtained and listed in Table 1. The four MOFs feature a similar Langmuir surface area of around $2000~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ and the pore sizes ranged within $2{-}4~\mathrm{nm}$ .
Herein, our lab-made resonant microcantilevers with $1.5\:\mathrm{Hz}/\$ pg mass-sensitivity are used as gravimetric sensing devices.31 Both the electrothermal resonance excitation element and the frequency signal readout piezoresistive Wheatstone-bridge are integrated in the silicon cantilever. Thanks to the MEMS batch fabrication, the used microcantilevers are highly uniform in mass-sensitivity and fundamental bending-mode resonant frequency. To form the xylene gas sensor, the MOF material is precisely loaded onto the sensing area (i.e., the free end) of the microcantilever. Equipped with an optical microscope, a commercial available inkjet printer is used to deposit the MOF material onto the designated sensing-region. Before depositing, the MOF material is ultrasonically dispersed into deionized (DI) water to form the ink. In situ manipulated under the microscope, several drops of the volume known MOF dispersion are deposited onto the cantilever. After the solvent of DI water is evaporated completely, the MOF-loaded resonant-gravimetric cantilever sensor is used for xylene detection. Figure 4 shows the SEM image of the fabricated resonant-gravimetric cantilever sensor, where the MOF sensing material of HKUST-1 crystals has been precisely deposited at the free-end of the cantilever.
In order to compare the xylene sensing performance of the four MOF sensing materials, the four MOFs are sequentially used to detect $p$ -xylene vapor under the same detection condition. Figure 5 summarizes the detection results of the four
MOF-loaded sensors to $p$ -xylene vapor with identical concentration of $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ . It can be clearly seen that HKUST1 exhibits the highest sensing response among the four MOFs. Among HKUST-1, MOF-5, and MOF-177, which are all with similar carboxyl-based linker, HKUST-1 is constructed with ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ units and, thus, has more moderate Lewis acidity than MOF-5 and MOF-177 (both with $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ as building blocks). Hence, HKUST-1 is more suitable for $p$ -xylene adsorbing and sensing. Compared with ZIF-8 that has zinc imidazolate structure, the benzene ring structural HKUST-1 should be a better material to detect xylene, due to the “like dissolves like” interaction effect. Therefore, HKUST-1 is reasonably chosen as the $p$ -xylene sensing material. Its more sensing parameters, including sensitivity, selectivity, and stability, will be further evaluated as follows.
Figure 6a shows the room-temperature tested real-time sensing curve of the HKUST-1 to $p$ -xylene vapors with various concentrations of $0.5{-}250~\mathrm{ppm}$ . For example, the HKUST-1 based sensor outputs remarkable frequency-shift response of 50 $\mathrm{Hz}$ to ${\mathsf{s o}}{\mathsf{p p m}}p$ -xylene vapor. Also shown in the inset of Figure 6a, the sensor outputs $0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ response to the trace-level $p{\cdot}$ - xylene vapor of $400\ \mathrm{ppb}$ (parts per billion by volume). The 0.1 $\mathrm{Hz}$ sensing signal is much higher than the noise-floor of about $\pm0.01$ Hz. Thus, we conclude that the HKUST-1 based $p{\cdot}$ - xylene sensor features the very fine limit of detection (LOD) of $400\ \mathrm{\ppb},$ which is already lower than the human olfactory threshold of $470\ \mathrm{ppb}$ . By transforming the real-time sensing data in Figure 6a, we obtain the relationship between sensing response and $p$ -xylene concentration and plot it in Figure 6b. In the low concentration range, the relationship can be well fitted into the Langmuir equation as
$$
Q=a\theta={\frac{a K p}{1+K p}}
$$
where $\boldsymbol{Q}$ is the quantity of the adsorbed xylene molecules (herein represented by the mass sensing response), $\theta$ is the fractional coverage that is proportional to the quantity of adsorbed xylene molecules, $p$ is the gas partial pressure (i.e., molecule concentration), $K$ is the equilibrium constant, and $a$ is the proportional coefficient. Based on Langmuir sorption theory, at the very low concentration range, the fractional coverage $\theta\ll1$ (i.e., $K p\ll1\AA.$ ), there exists a linear relationship of
$$
Q=a K p
$$
In this study, the MOF sensing material of HKUST-1 can output an approximately linear response to xylene vapor where the concentration is in the range of $\leq10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ (adjusted $R\mathrm{.}$ square is $98.6\%$ ). When HKUST-1 is exposed to higher $p{\cdot}$ - xylene concentrations than $10\:\mathrm{ppm}$ , the sensor response follows the Langmuir adsorption curve defined by eq 1.
To investigate the selectivity of HKUST-1 to $p$ -xylene, nine kinds of common vapors are chosen as the interfering gases, including ethanol, methanol, acetone, formaldehyde, ether, acetone, ammonia, benzene, toluene, and ethylbenzene. Figure 6c shows the experimentally obtained selectivity evaluation results of the HKUST-1 based $p$ -xylene sensor. When $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ interfering gases of ethanol, acetone, ammonia, ether, or formaldehyde exposed to the sensor, the yielded very low sensor response is negligible, indicating satisfactory selectivity to these interfering gases. However, to the other two interfering vapors of benzene, toluene, and ethylbenzene, the sensor outputs considerable responses. The reason lies in that, just like xylene, the molecules of benzene or toluene also provide similar host−guest interaction with the HKUST-1 sensing material. Due to the smaller molecular weight of benzene or toluene than xylene, the sensor outputs lower response to benzene or toluene than $p$ -xylene of identical concentration. In general, the HKUST-1 based sensor can be utilized for detecting of benzene homologues that are also generally categorized as BTEX (i.e., benzene, toluene, ethylbenzene, and xylene).32−35 Not only $p{\cdot}$ - xylene but also the other two xylene isomers of o-xylene and mxylene have been examined together by using the HKUST-1 based sensor. As is shown in Figure 6c, the sensor shows the highest response to $p$ -xylene, and the order of sensing response to the three isomers is as $p$ -xylene $\approx o$ -xylene $>m$ -xylene. Since $m$ -xylene features lower activation than $p$ -xylene and $o$ -xylene, the adsorption capacity of the HKUST-1 crystals to $m$ -xylene should be lower than that to the other two isomers of xylene. Due to that $p$ -xylene has similar activation to $o$ -xylene, the HKUST-1 based sensor exhibits similar response (i.e., sensitivity) to these two isomers. Actually, $p$ -xylene is the mostly produced and the widest used chemical product among the three xylene isomers; thereby, it is selected as the representative compound of xylene in this research.
To assess repeatability of the HKUST-1 coated cantilever sensor, the same sensor is repeatedly introduced to $p$ -xylene vapor of an identical concentration of $8\ \mathrm{ppm}$ . The three-time response signals are recorded in Figure 7a. The three-time response signals are always as $28.5\pm0.3\mathrm{\Hz}$ . The relative standard deviation (RSD) of $1.1\%$ $\left(n=3\right)$ ) indicates satisfactory detection repeatability of the MOF-based resonant gas sensor.
Figure 7b shows two sensing results of the same sensor to $p$ - xylene vapor, which are obtained with a time interval of two months. Compared with the first-time detection to $10\ {\mathrm{ppm}}\ p.$ - xylene, after two months, the sensor outputs a slightly lower sensing response (difference $<4\%$ ) to the gas of identical concentration, indicating good repeatability and stability of the sensor. The very slight decrease of sensitivity is possibly caused by nonspecific adsorption of the HKUST-1 crystals to interfering molecules like $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in an ambient atmosphere. We have also found that, even when the sensitivity is decreased after long-term usage or storage, the sensing performance of the HKUST-1 based sensor can be effectively recovered by using simple treatment like heating activation or UV-light radiation. In order to further verify the stability of the HKUST-1 based sensor, XRD is used to characterize the crystal-structure of the HKUST-1 sensing material before and after $p$ -xylene detection, with the results shown in Figure 8. Compared with the blank HKUST-1 sample, no difference can be observed in the XRD pattern of the same sample after detection to $p$ -xylene vapor. In this research, the detected $p$ -xylene is at low concentration of ppm level. According to the XRD results, low dose $p$ -xylene has negligible negative effect on the structure stability of the HKUST-1 crystals. Based on the sensing measurement in Figure 7b and the XRD analysis, it can be proved that the HKUST-1 based sensor features good long-term stability.
In order to elucidate the adsorbing/sensing mechanism of HKUST-1 to $p$ -xylene, in situ diffuse reflectance infrared Fourier transform spectroscopy (DRIFTS) is utilized to investigate the interaction between the HKUST-1 crystal and the $p$ -xylene guest molecule. Figure 9 plots and compares the DRIFTS results of HKUST-1 before and after $p$ -xylene introduction. Due to the electron-rich characteristic of the $p{\cdot}$ - xylene molecule, the adsorbed $p$ -xylene molecules will enhance the electron-donor property of the benzene ring in the BTC linker of HKUST-1 and, thus, will strongly influence the infrared adsorption bands of the HKUST-1 crystal. The in situ DRIFTS results indicate that the characteristic peaks of HKUST-1 are indeed affected by the adsorbed xylene molecules. For example, one peak at $1633~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ , which is assigned to asymmetric stretching vibration of the $-\mathrm{COO^{-}}$ group of the BTC linker, will shift toward a lower wavenumber after the MOF material is exposed to $p$ -xylene. Such a blue-shift phenomenon can also be observed at another peak located at
$1449~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}.$ , which is attributed to the symmetric stretching vibration of $-\mathrm{COO^{-}}$ group. In the DRIFTS characterization, the blue-shift phenomenon is induced by the weakened bondforce constant. In this research, due to the adsorbed $p$ -xylene molecules, the electron-donor property of the benzene-ring is enhanced and, thus, the xylene-adsorbed benzene-ring can weaken the bond-force constant of the neighboring $-\mathrm{COO^{-}}$ group in the BTC linker. Therefore, the in situ DRIFTS characterization confirms that $p$ -xylene molecules can be adsorbed on HKUST-1 crystals for mass-type resonantgravimetric sensing.
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, $p$ -xylene sensing performances of MOF materials have been systematically investigated by using the massgravimetric sensing platform of resonant microcantilevers. According to our research, the typical MOF material of HKUST-1 crystal (with Langmuir surface area above $2000\mathrm{m}^{2}/$ g) shows satisfactory sensing performance to trace $p$ -xylene vapor. The HKUST-1 based sensor shows a Langmuir-type response to $p$ -xylene vapor within the concentration range of $0.4{-}250\mathrm{ppm}$ . The LOD of the HKUST-1 based sensor reaches $400\ \mathrm{\ppb}$ that is already lower than the human olfactory threshold of $470\ \mathrm{ppb}$ . The sensor also shows good selectivity, repeatability and long-term stability. For the first time, this research declares that MOF materials exhibit potential usage for detection to the chemically inert $p$ -xylene.
